Bear in mind too, each time you are granted a
gift, your prayer timeout becomes positive, so
the next N sacrifices (until you "feel reconciled")
don't count for gifting purposes.

As far as the math, assuming your base chance
to receive a gift for each sacrifice is 1/x and your
prayer timeout is zero, the chance of NOT
receiving a gift when you sacrifice once is (x-1)/x,
and thus the chance of not receiving anything
after k sacrifices is ((x-1)/x)^k, where k is an
integer, under the assumption that x does not
change.  (x does change if you receive a gift...
but I think we can ignore that wrinkle, as long
as we're not trying to calculate the mean or
median expected number of gifts.)

If your calculation of 1/26 gift chance per
sacrifice is correct, then your chance of not
receiving any gifts after e.g. 50 sacrifices
would be (25/26)^50, which is about 14%.
For 100 sacrifices, the chance of receiving
no gift is more like 2%.  (Note that the
chance of receiving no gift after each
individual sacrifice is still 25/26.  It's only
the collective probability that goes up with
an increased number of sacrifices.)

Again, this is only counting sacrifices where
your prayer timeout is zero.  Any time you
have a non-zero prayer timeout, those
sacrifices don't count toward gifts.  After
Frost Brand was given, for example, you
presumably sacrificed several corpses just
to get your prayer timeout back down to
zero.  There was no chance of receiving
a gift from those.  Similarly, any time you
pray or wish, you get a prayer timeout.

Note too that NetHack has so many random
events in it, it is pretty much a sure thing
that in any given game *something* with
much less than a 1% chance of happening
will happen.  It's just not the same unlikely
something each time.


